// Operations over `Ref`, and a note about their names.
//
// A bang name can only ever be *used*: the parser refuses one at every position
// where a name is bound, so nothing below can be called `modify!` however much
// it would like to be. `ref!`, `get!` and `set!` are seeded term bindings, not
// keywords, and that rule is what reserves the spelling rather than a list of
// builtins anywhere.
//
// `replace` rather than `swap` for a duller reason: requiring is textual and
// flat, so every module shares one namespace and a later binding shadows an
// earlier one *silently*. `data/pair.ga` already has a `swap`.
#require "data/bool.ga"
#require "rec/fix.ga"

// The cell settles `A` before the function is looked at, so a bare lambda
// reaches it knowing its own parameter.
let modify = fn [A](r: Ref[A], f: (A) -> A) -> set!(r, f(get!(r)))

let replace = fn [A](r: Ref[A], v: A) ->
  let old = get!(r)
  let _ = set!(r, v)
  old

// Landin's knot: a cell holding a function that reads the cell is general
// recursion, with no `Rec` datatype anywhere. `rec/fix.ga` gets there through a
// negative recursive datatype instead; this is the other way in, and between
// them they are why `docs/roadmap.md` says the evaluator needs a fuel counter
// whatever else it does.
//
// The seed is the whole difficulty. A cell has to hold something before it
// holds the answer, so tying a knot this way is a *synthesis* problem the type
// system hands to the author: produce an `(A) -> B` when the only `(A) -> B` in
// the program is the one being defined. `fixFrom` ducks it by taking the seed
// as a parameter, which is honest where the seed is meant -- iterating from an
// approximation -- and evasive where it is not.
let fixFrom = fn [A, B](seed: (A) -> B, f: ((A) -> B) -> (A) -> B) ->
  let r = ref!(seed)
  let g = fn (v: A) -> get!(r)(v)
  let _ = set!(r, f(g))
  g

// Seeded with bottom instead, which is what makes the knot a closed term. This
// is `fix` again, to the letter -- `[A, B](((A) -> B) -> (A) -> B) -> (A) -> B`
// -- reached through mutation rather than through `Rec`.
//
// The seed has to say its type. Nothing in the argument list constrains it, so
// its two variables solve at `unknown -> never` and `set!` then refuses the
// real function; the cell learns what it holds one line too late. Same shape as
// `toList` in `uses-both.ga`, and the same fix.
//
// What the bottom seed buys is small and worth naming. A read before the
// backpatch used to answer the seed, and no type said it was a placeholder;
// now it diverges. That trades a wrong answer for no answer, which is better,
// but it is still not a type error -- the obligation only moved, from the
// author to whatever later proves the seed is dead. Under a store effect that
// proof is an aliasing question, where a `rec` binder would have made it a
// scoping one. It is the argument for the group node in `docs/roadmap.md` §3,
// and the argument against desugaring one to this.
let fixKnot = fn [A, B](f: ((A) -> B) -> (A) -> B) ->
  let r = ref!(diverge[A, B]())
  let g = fn (v: A) -> get!(r)(v)
  let _ = set!(r, f(g))
  g
